Implemented server-sent mods end to end.

**Server**

- Each server can now have a `servermods/` folder next to its config: `./servermods/` for the main server, `worlds/<name>/servermods/` for additional servers.
- [serverMods.js](src/js/net/minecraft/server/serverMods.js) scans mod folders, reads `ModData.js`, and packages text files plus base64 PNG/OGG files.
- [ServerModLoader.js](src/js/net/minecraft/server/ServerModLoader.js) registers each mod’s blocks, items, and commands in the server’s own registry, so placement, block ticks, and commands work server-side.
- The payload is sent immediately after the join packet and before chunks, and again when `/server` switches worlds.
- Config in [serverconfig.example.conf](serverconfig.example.conf):
  ```conf
  serverMods = true
  serverModsDir = "servermods"
  ```

**Client**

- [ModLoader.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/ModLoader.js) loads server mods through the existing in-memory mod pipeline. They are never written to `mods/`.
- [NetworkManager.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/network/NetworkManager.js) holds binary packets until mod registration finishes, so chunk block IDs are decoded against the new blocks.
- Leaving the server calls `unloadServerMods()`, which removes blocks/items, frees texture-atlas slots, deletes GUI textures, recipes, smelting recipes, tabs, commands, render hooks, and cached modules.
- [BlockRegistry.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/block/BlockRegistry.js) now also clears `Block.getById()` entries on unregister, which is required for blocks to actually disappear.
- The unload is generation-guarded, so disconnecting mid-load cannot leak half-registered mods into the menu or another server.

**Checks**

- `npx vite build` passes.
- Node smoke test with a temporary `servermods/factorio`: loaded 2 blocks, confirmed `factorio:conveyor` existed, then unloaded and confirmed both `BlockRegistry` and `Block.getById()` were empty.
- No live multiplayer client session was run.

