Both fixed. I stopped the server I had started and closed its tab — no game process is running from me, and I did the rest by reading code and running pure-module checks rather than launching anything.

**The `Start.js:10` error was a real import cycle**, unrelated to scaffolding. `Minecraft.js` imports `NetworkManager.js`, which imported `{ require }` from `Start.js`, which imports `Minecraft.js`: `Start → Minecraft → NetworkManager → Start`. When the module graph is evaluated in an order where `Start`'s body runs before `Minecraft`'s class declaration, line 10's `Minecraft.VERSION` hits the temporal dead zone and throws exactly what you saw. The `require` helper only reads `window[module]`, so it never needed to live on the entry point:

- New leaf module [Require.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/network/util/Require.js) holds it.
- [NetworkManager.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/network/NetworkManager.js#L4), [Authentication.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/network/util/Authentication.js#L2) and [CryptManager.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/network/util/CryptManager.js#L3) now import it from there, so nothing under `src/js` imports `Start.js` any more (verified by grep — zero matches).
- [Start.js](src/js/Start.js#L76) still re-exports `require` for anything else that expected it there, which no longer participates in the cycle.

**Walking on scaffold** needed a collision box that is solid from above but not a wall from the side. A plain top slab doesn't work here: the engine only auto-steps obstacles half a block high, so a 2-pixel plate one block up would read as an unsteppable wall at ground level and you couldn't walk into a scaffold at all. Instead, collision queries now carry how the entity is moving:

- [Entity.js:206](src/js/net/minecraft/client/entity/Entity.js#L206) passes `downward: targetY < 0` on the vertical sweep.
- [World.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/World.js#L372) forwards the swept region plus that flag into a new per-block hook.
- [Block.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/block/Block.js#L289) adds `getCollisionBoundingBoxFor(...)`, defaulting to the old behaviour, so no other block changes.
- [BlockScaffolding.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/block/type/BlockScaffolding.js#L48) returns a thin plate (`0.875–1.0`) for downward sweeps only, and for sideways/step sweeps only once your feet are at or above the plate.

So a scaffold platform catches you when you land on it, you can jump or climb up through it, and walking into one at ground level still passes straight through and lets you climb as before.

Verified with `node --check` on all ten touched modules, and by exercising the new collision hook directly in Node (pure modules, no engine): ground-level sideways → `null`, standing on top sideways → plate, falling → plate, climbing up → `null`, feet inside the block → `null`, and the textures resolve to `scaffolding_top` / `scaffolding_bottom` / `scaffolding_side`. I have not seen it render or walked on it in-game, since that needs the game running.
