The cause: the top plate answered every downward collision, so standing on a scaffold and pressing sneak still had the plate caught under your feet — you could only descend once your body was already inside the block, where the climb logic takes over.

The fix passes the sneak state into the collision query and lets scaffolding ignore its plate entirely while sneaking:

- [Entity.js:209](src/js/net/minecraft/client/entity/Entity.js#L209) sends `sneaking: this.isSneaking()` with the vertical sweep (the sneak state is set from the crouch key earlier in the same tick, in `PlayerEntity.updateEntityActionState`, before `travel()`).
- [World.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/World.js#L383) forwards it in the query.
- [BlockScaffolding.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/block/type/BlockScaffolding.js#L48) returns `null` when sneaking, so nothing catches you.

So: shift on top → you drop through the plate → the framework's climb flag (`isClimbable`) then holds you at the fixed descending speed through the rest of the column. Release shift and the plate is back, so you settle/hover in place; walk or jump off to leave.

