Each part is drawn with the block's own face textures (`["block", 326, box]`), so the plate shows `scaffolding_top` from above and `scaffolding_bottom` underneath, while the posts and plate edges show `scaffolding_side`. Because [renderFace](src/js/net/minecraft/client/render/BlockRenderer.js#L584-L620) derives UVs from a part's world-space size rather than stretching the whole texture, each part samples the slice of the texture it would have had in a full face — the posts pick up the frame's vertical border columns, the plate edges pick up the thick top band, so the parts line up as one continuous frame.

One thing had to change to make this safe: the world treats multipart parts as the block's collision shape (that's how fences and stairs work), which would have turned the frame into six solid boxes and killed both the walk-through and the climb. So [Block.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/block/Block.js#L140) now has `usesMultipartShape()` (default `true`), and [World.js](src/js/net/minecraft/client/world/World.js#L264) plus `collisionRayTrace` only use parts when it returns true. Scaffolding returns `false`, keeping its previous behaviour: parts are visual only, collision is still the one-way top plate, and the whole cube stays clickable.

